  "history_summaries": [
    "Round 1/3: Fix Controller READY→BUSY unconditional transition (dut_bug). Failing properties: Main.ackA_implies_reqA, Main.ackB_implies_reqB. Root cause: In Controller class (arbiter.scala:89-92), the is(ControllerState.READY) block unconditionally transitions to BUSY and asserts ackReg, ignoring whether isSelected is still true. This allows a spurious acknowledgment when the arbiter deselects the controller between the READY→BUSY clock edges. Fix: wrap the BUSY transition in when(isSelected) and add an .otherwise branch that resets to IDLE without asserting ackReg. This is a DUT bug fix targeting the Controller FSM shared by all three controller instances (A, B, C). All three original assertion labels (ackA implies reqA, ackB implies reqB, ackC implies reqC) are preserved unchanged.",
    "Round 2/3: Fix Controller IDLE passToken liveness bug (dut_bug). Failing property: Main.reqA_eventually_gets_ack_when_active. Root cause: In Controller class (arbiter.scala ~line 99), the IDLE state `.otherwise` (not selected) branch unconditionally drops passTokenReg to false.B, even when the client has a pending request. This causes all three passTokens to go low, making active=0, which stalls the arbiter (sel=X) and prevents the requesting controller from ever being selected again — a permanent deadlock. Fix: wrap the `passTokenReg := false.B` in `when(io.req) { passTokenReg := true.B } .otherwise { passTokenReg := false.B }` so that a controller with a pending request keeps the system active until the arbiter cycles back to select it. This addresses the causal path (liveness stall through passToken→active→arbiter sel→isSelected), not just the waveform symptom. All six original assertion labels (including the failing reqA_eventually_gets_ack_when_active) are preserved unchanged.",
    "Round 3/3: Fix Controller FSM to skip READY state (dut_bug). Failing property: Main.reqA_eventually_gets_ack_when_active. Root cause: The arbiter changes selection every clock cycle (A→B→C→A→...), but the Controller required two consecutive cycles of isSelected (IDLE→READY, then READY→BUSY/ack). Since the arbiter never holds a selection for two cycles, the controller always fell back from READY to IDLE without asserting ack, permanently starving the client. Fix: In Controller class (arbiter.scala IDLE state, ~line 90), change the transition from `state := ControllerState.READY` to `state := ControllerState.BUSY; ackReg := true.B`, completing the handshake in the single cycle the arbiter provides. This addresses the causal path: the single-cycle arbiter selection is a protocol invariant, so the handshake design must match it. The READY state handler is preserved as dead-code safety. All six original assertion labels (including the failing reqA_eventually_gets_ack_when_active) are preserved unchanged."
  ],
